UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 16, Lesson 1: Space, time and energy', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Dance elements include space (where you move), time (speed, rhythm, stillness) and energy (how the movement feels: sharp, smooth, strong, light). Changing one element transforms the same steps. Safe practice means controlled landings, clear personal space and stopping on the signal.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nTake four walking steps. Describe one change in space, one in time and one in energy.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Space: curve pathway. Time: slow then freeze. Energy: light footsteps.'' All three elements named.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking dance is only fast tricks. Stillness and light energy are valid choices.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nChoreograph eight counts changing space, time and energy once each. Perform and label the three changes for a viewer.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Dance elements include space (where you move), time (speed, rhythm, stillness) and energy (how the movement feels: sharp, smooth, strong, light). Changing one element transforms the same steps. Safe practice means controlled landings, clear personal space and stopping on the signal.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DAN-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Choreograph eight counts changing space, time and energy once each. Perform and label the three changes for a viewer. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Space: curve pathway. Time: slow then freeze. Energy: light footsteps.'' All three elements named. Reasoning: I used space by attending to time rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking dance is only fast tricks. Stillness and light energy are valid choices. Correction: Strong answer: ''Space: curve pathway. Time: slow then freeze. Energy: light footsteps.'' All three elements named. Why: the correction restores accurate use of space.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking dance is only fast tricks. Stillness and light energy are valid choices. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating space on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with space, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Space: curve pathway. Time: slow then freeze. Energy: light footsteps.'' All three elements named. This matches the lesson skill for space.","options":["Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Space: curve pathway. Time: slow then freeze. Energy: light footsteps.'' All three elements named.","Thinking dance is only fast tricks. Stillness and light energy are valid choices.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Take four walking steps. Describe one change in space, one in time and one in energy. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Thinking dance is only fast tricks. Stillness and light energy are valid choices. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Dance elements include space (where you move), time (speed, rhythm, stillness) and energy (how the movement feels: sharp, smooth, strong, light). Changing one…","Strong answer: ''Space: curve pathway. Time: slow then freeze. Energy: light footsteps.'' All three elements named.","Careful use of time with clear evidence.","Thinking dance is only fast tricks. Stillness and light energy are valid choices."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising space?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named time, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the time used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising space. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Space: curve pathway. Time: slow then freeze. Energy: light footsteps.'' All three elements named. uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Thinking dance is only fast tricks. Stillness and light energy are valid choices.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Space: curve pathway. Time: slow then freeze. Energy: light footsteps.'' All three elements named."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about space?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Thinking dance is only fast tricks. Stillness and light energy are valid choices.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about space.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about time?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Example: The same arm sweep performed slowly with light energy feels gentle; performed quickly with strong energy feels bold. Students…","Thinking dance is only fast tricks. Stillness and light energy are valid choices.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Dance elements include space (where you move), time (speed, rhythm, stillness) and energy (how the movement… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Thinking dance is only fast tricks. Stillness and light energy are valid choices.","Choreograph eight counts changing space, time and energy once each. Perform and label the three changes for a viewer."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of space?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating space on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Space: curve pathway. Time: slow then freeze. Energy: light footsteps.'' All three elements named. Reasoning: I used space by attending to time rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with space, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking dance is only fast tricks. Stillness and light energy are valid choices. Correction: Strong answer: ''Space: curve pathway. Time: slow then freeze. Energy: light footsteps.'' All three elements named. Why: the correction restores accurate use of space.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y4-w16-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise space.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Dance","success_criteria":["I can use space with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify time and explain why it matters.","I can practise energy independently on a new prompt.","I can notice dynamic needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Dance elements include space (where you move), time (speed, rhythm, stillness) and energy (how the movement feels: sharp, smooth, strong, light). Changing one element transforms the same steps. Safe practice means controlled landings, clear personal space and stopping on the…","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Dance elements include space (where you move), time (speed, rhythm, stillness) and energy (how the movement feels: sharp, smooth, strong, light). Changing one element transforms the same steps. Safe practice means controlled landings, clear personal space and stopping on the signal.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about space?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: The same arm sweep performed slowly with light energy feels gentle; performed quickly with strong energy feels bold. Students compare both versions.\n\nTRY FIRST\nTake four walking steps. Describe one change in space, one in time and one in energy.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Space: curve pathway. Time: slow then freeze. Energy: light footsteps.'' All three elements named.","explain":"Dance elements include space (where you move), time (speed, rhythm, stillness) and energy (how the movement feels: sharp, smooth, strong, light). Changing one element transforms the same steps. Safe practice means controlled landings, clear personal space and stopping on the signal.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking dance is only fast tricks. Stillness and light energy are valid choices. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Dance elements.","caption":"Change one at a time.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Space</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">where</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Time</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">speed stillness</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: The same arm sweep performed slowly with light energy feels gentle; performed quickly with strong energy feels  Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: The same arm sweep performed slowly with light energy feels gentle; performed quickly with strong energy feels  Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about space?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: The same arm sweep performed slowly with light energy feels gentle; performed quickly with strong energy feels bold. Students compare both versions. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Energy and safety.","caption":"Control landings.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Energy</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">how it feels</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Safe stop</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">on signal</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about space?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking dance is only fast tricks. Stillness and light energy are valid choices. Here is a more helpful example: Example: The same arm sweep performed slowly with light energy feels gentle; performed quickly with strong energy feels bold. Students compare both versions.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Space: curve pathway. Time: slow then freeze. Energy: light footsteps.'' All three elements named.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking dance is only fast tricks. Stillness and light energy are valid choices. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about space?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDance elements include space (where you move), time (speed, rhythm, stillness) and energy (how the movement feels: sharp, smooth, strong, light). Changing one element transforms the same steps. Safe practice means controlled landings, clear personal space and stopping on the signal. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 16, Lesson 1: Space, time and energy","unit_number":4,"unit_title":"Space, time, energy and choreography","week":16,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: The same arm sweep performed slowly with light energy feels gentle; performed quickly with strong energy feels bold. Students compare both versions.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Dance elements.","caption":"Change one at a time.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Space</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">where</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Time</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">speed stillness</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Energy and safety.","caption":"Control landings.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Energy</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">how it feels</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Safe stop</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">on signal</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 4"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y4-w16-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
